From 99ba5fee6841ec3f983cd67cedb5d09780025c03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:32:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] common: the PS4's emulated DRAM on demand, and a GPU budget
 that follows it

The Switch's 4 GiB of DRAM took 4 GiB of the PS4's 4.5 GiB of direct memory
up front, leaving the GPU about 480 MiB. It now comes in 2 MiB chunks as the
game first touches them (a fault handler maps each, zeroed elsewhere first
so that no thread sees it half cleared), and a zero clear of chunks never
touched (a new heap) leaves them alone. EDEN_PS4_DRAM_ON_DEMAND=0 takes it
all up front as before. IOFile touches a large buffer before the C library
hands it to read() or write(): the kernel's accesses don't fault into the
handler, they fail.

The caches' GPU usage on the PS4 is now what the GPU's buffers hold (the
winsys's count), scaled against what is free plus that, less a 384 MiB
reserve for the DRAM to grow into: their garbage collection tightens as the
game's memory grows and hands direct memory back to it.

dynarmic's POSIX exception handler is no longer built for the PS4 (no
fastmem there): with no code registered it logged every fault, from a
signal handler, before passing it on.
---
